      // [src_ameghino_britannica, src_ameghino_matthew, src_ameghino_menendez | 1880-1911 | reference entry, obituary, scholarly review] Britannica says he advanced radical theories, including that mammals and humans originated on the Argentine pampas; Matthew calls him a 'daring speculator' who maintained theoretical conclusions most paleontologists could not accept and sought endorsement abroad when it failed at home; Menendez says he built his own primate phylogeny from comparative anatomy and pursued the origin hypothesis through his career. SELF-CALIBRATION (agent): reviewed as a top-risk row; lowered from 72 / 0.52 to 68 / 0.48 because the evidence shows heterodox conclusions defended against consensus rather than the quality of independent reasoning, and Matthew and Britannica both call parts of the work hasty or isolated.
