      conflict_tolerance: [68, 0.5, "s", "D"],
      // [src_ameghino_vizcaino, src_ameghino_gondwana | 1888-1895 | scholarly article, blog] Vizcaino reports intense competition for Patagonian fossils with Moreno's museum and that in January 1893 Florentino tried to publish ahead of Mercerat on supposed monotreme remains; the blog compares the Moreno feud to the Cope-Marsh rivalry. SELF-CALIBRATION (agent): reviewed as a top-risk row; lowered from 66 / 0.46 to 62 / 0.42 because it rests on one dated priority race plus a popular blog's analogy, and Vizcaino also records a good disposition toward foreign scientists.
      competitiveness: [62, 0.42, "i", "D"],
      // [src_ameghino_matthew, src_ameghino_vizcaino, src_ameghino_gondwana | 1887-1902 | obituary, scholarly article, blog] Matthew and Vizcaino agree that the brothers divided the work, with Carlos exploring Patagonia and Florentino describing and publishing the finds; Vizcaino adds that Florentino urged Carlos to publish in prestigious journals and told him to share information with the American paleontologist Hatcher in 1896. The partnership is well described, but it was a family arrangement, the letters are known through a 1935 edition, and the other documented relationships (Moreno, Mercerat, Burmeister) were adversarial.
      collaboration: [62, 0.48, "i", "A"],
      // [src_ameghino_matthew, src_ameghino_vizcaino, src_ameghino_gondwana | 1878-1902 | obituary, scholarly article, blog] Matthew says he earned the money for the expeditions from a small stationer's shop in La Plata and took his collection to the Paris Exposition of 1878; Vizcaino documents the sale of fossils, a loan, Cordoba Academy funding and a government subscription for the 1889 monograph, and the blog adds the 1902 plan of casts and exchanges to keep original fossils in Argentina. Several distinct ways of financing research are documented in sources that agree; the blog items are low-weight.
