    directoryVisible: true,
    sources: [{ id: "src_aggrey_wikidata", kind: "wikidata", title: "James Emman Kwegyir Aggrey (Q1679925), verified live via the Wikidata API (2026-10-31): English description 'Gold Coast-born intellectual, missionary, and teacher (1875-1927)', born 1875-10-18, died 1927-07-30, no Korean label. Orientation only; not used as support for any row.", url: "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1679925" }, { id: "src_aggrey_dacb", kind: "biography", title: "Mark R. Lipschutz and R. Kent Rasmussen, 'Aggrey, James Emman Kwegyir (1875-1927)', Dictionary of African Historical Biography, 2nd edition (University of California Press, 1986), reproduced with permission by the Dictionary of African Christian Biography at Boston University, read in full: Wesleyan schooling and teaching in mission schools before 1898, B.A. at Livingstone College in 1902, teaching on its faculty, doctoral work at Columbia, membership of the Phelps-Stokes education commissions (tours of 1920 and 1924, the only African member), senior staff of Achimota College from late 1924, sudden death in July 1927. CANONICAL SIGNIFICANCE SOURCE (Britannica returned HTTP 403). An independent scholarly dictionary entry; it notes that his long absence from Ghana caused difficulties and offers no other critical assessment.", url: "https://dacb.org/stories/ghana/aggrey-james/" }, { id: "src_aggrey_smith", kind: "biography", title: "Edwin W. Smith, 'Aggrey of Africa: A Study in Black and White' (Doubleday, Doran and Company, New York, 1929; Internet Archive scan, full text read in part), a biography written for the Phelps-Stokes trustees from his letters, his widow and colleagues: his schooling, his student years and professorship at Livingstone College (registrar, classes in English, sociology and economics), the two commissions, and the genesis of Achimota, including his recommendation of A. G. Fraser as principal and his appointment as assistant vice-principal. The author says he tried not to paint an idealised picture, but the book is admiring and much of its testimony comes from friends; it also records the 'running fire of black criticism' he met from radical Africans at one meeting.", url: "https://archive.org/details/aggreyofafricast0000edwi" }, { id: "src_aggrey_sotiropoulos", kind: "institution", title: "Karen Sotiropoulos, 'James and Rose Aggrey and the Black Atlantic Gestations of African Nationalism', International Journal of African Historical Studies 53(2), 2020, pp. 239-264, abstract read on the Cleveland State University repository page (full text not opened): calls him the father of African education and the 'Booker T. Washington of Africa', says he influenced later nationalists, and reads his vision of cooperation on the Phelps-Stokes tour through his letters to his wife. A modern scholarly, nuancing account; abstract only, so used for context and for the political reading that is not scored.", url: "https://engagedscholarship.csuohio.edu/clhist_facpub/137" }, { id: "src_aggrey_saho", kind: "institution", title: "South African History Online, 'James Emmanuel Kwegyir Aggrey' (first published 17 February 2011, updated 20 June 2018), read: born in Anamabu on 18 October 1875, spent three months in South Africa in 1921 as a member of the Phelps-Stokes Commission, a prime mover in founding the Johannesburg Joint Council of Europeans and Africans, and with a race-relations outlook of compromise and cooperation that reinforced the tendency of African National Congress leaders to join interracial councils. An independent history archive; its race-relations material is political context and is not scored.", url: "https://sahistory.org.za/node/7776" }],
    rows: {
      // [src_aggrey_smith, src_aggrey_dacb, src_aggrey_saho | 1920-1925 | admiring biography + dictionary entry + history archive] The biography reports that he held audiences of six or seven hundred students spellbound and that a Sunday address ran so long with questions that it barely finished by midnight, quoting a colleague at Fort Hare on his practical, well-aimed addresses; the dictionary says that as the only African on the commission he drew strong interest from African audiences. The history archive notes that his emphasis on compromise shaped how interracial councils were viewed, and the biography records a 'running fire of black criticism' from radical Africans at one meeting, so the trait is recorded as dual-edged. Scored for his lecturing on education; his race-relations advocacy is political context and not scored.
      persuasiveness: [70, 0.5, "s", "D"],
      // [src_aggrey_smith | 1898-1920 | admiring biography] The biography records that as student and then registrar at Livingstone College he called the roll himself at eight o'clock, was a stickler for punctuality (students tried and failed to catch him late), was always accessible, worked hard and expected students to do likewise, and won a gold medal for scholarship and deportment. SELF-CALIBRATION (agent): lowered from 64 / 0.44 because the daily-routine detail comes from one admiring biography relaying colleagues' recollections, with no independent source on his work habits.
      discipline: [60, 0.4, "s", "A"],
      // [src_aggrey_smith, src_aggrey_dacb | 1898-1927 | admiring biography + dictionary entry] He graduated B.A. at the head of his class in 1902, later took the A.M. and a doctorate of divinity, and while teaching resumed study at Columbia University toward a doctorate, going on leave in May 1927 to write the dissertation (dictionary entry); the biography adds that he won gold medals for English composition and scholarship and delivered the first Greek oration heard at the college. The degrees and the doctoral work are documented in two sources; the motive is not scored.
      mastery_orientation: [66, 0.48, "d", "A"],
      // [src_aggrey_smith, src_aggrey_dacb | 1914-1927 | admiring biography + dictionary entry] The dictionary says his friendship with T. Jesse Jones brought him onto the Phelps-Stokes commissions, and the biography describes the second commission as international, interdenominational and interracial, records that he told J. H. Oldham he could work with A. G. Fraser as principal of Achimota, and that Fraser named him first member of his staff; at Livingstone he encouraged a colleague's student orchestra. The sources are friendly and the working relationships are described by participants and an admiring biographer.
      collaboration: [64, 0.44, "s", "A"],
      // [src_aggrey_smith, src_aggrey_dacb | 1902-1920 | admiring biography + dictionary entry] At Livingstone College he was registrar and financial secretary and took classes in English language and literature, sociology and economics, later teaching New Testament Greek and Christian sociology at the seminary, while serving as a rural pastor (dictionary entry) and, as a student, contributing articles to a daily newspaper (biography). The teaching breadth is documented; the quality of each strand is not scored.
      cross_domain_range: [58, 0.42, "d", "A"],
    },
